{"answerText":"NYC Planning’s supplied official amendment-history metadata for ZR § 42-00 identifies 12 events: the section was added effective **December 15, 1961** (CP15820) and amended effective **August 15, 1974** (CP22683); **September 19, 1985** (N850720ZRY); **October 25, 1993** (N930327CZRY); **October 25, 1995** (N950384ZRY); **September 9, 2004** (N040202ZRY); **February 2, 2011** (N110090AZRY); **October 10, 2013** (N130331AZRY); **December 6, 2023** (N230113ZRY); twice on **June 6, 2024** (N240010ZRY and N240011ZRY); and **December 5, 2024** (N240290ZRY). The metadata supplies a CPC report link for each event and short notes, including the 2024 reorganizations, A-suffix applicability, and cross-reference updates.

That is a supplied snapshot of official amendment-history metadata, not historical enacted text. Before reconstructing § 42-00 as in force on any particular date, a professional should obtain and compare the enacted text of the relevant pre- and post-amendment versions (including the linked CPC reports and enactment materials), confirm each amendment’s operative effective date and any transition/applicability language, and verify whether another amendment or special rule modified or superseded the provision for the date and circumstances at issue. The current text itself says that Chapter 2 use provisions may be modified or superseded by special rules in Article I, Article VI, and Special Purpose Districts (ZR § 42-00).

The current supplied text is from the continuously amended Zoning Resolution through August 13, 2026; it is not proof of what § 42-00 said on an earlier date.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Official metadata identifies the amendment-event sequence","explanation":"The supplied official NYC Planning amendment-history snapshot identifies one addition and 11 amendments for ZR § 42-00, gives each event’s effective date and CPC report number/link, and provides the listed project names or notes. This is metadata, not an enacted historical version of the section.","sectionID":"20017271","sourceIDs":["research-metadata-zoning-amendment-history-a0c7210b20faf0f53be44e8b"]},{"heading":"Current § 42-00 preserves potential special-rule modification or supersession","explanation":"The current enacted text states that the Chapter 2 use provisions may be modified or superseded by special rules for certain areas in Article I, Article VI, and Special Purpose Districts. Thus, the current general-provisions text itself does not establish a historical-date result without the pertinent historical text and applicable special-rule review.","sectionID":"20017271","sourceIDs":["1d64e29a-5afa-44b8-beb2-f4a7426af2c9"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":[],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied official metadata is a snapshot and identifies amendment events and report links only; it does not reproduce every historical version of ZR § 42-00 or establish the text in force on a selected historical date.","The supplied enacted text is continuously amended text through August 13, 2026. No historical enacted version, transition text, or amendment enactment text was supplied for a date-specific reconstruction."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["For the target date, the enacted version of ZR § 42-00 immediately before and after each relevant amendment, together with the enacted amendment materials and any effective-date or transition provisions.","The applicable historical special-rule text, if any, in Article I, Article VI, or a Special Purpose District that could have modified or superseded the general Chapter 2 use provisions for the circumstances being evaluated."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20017271","sourceIDs":["research-metadata-zoning-amendment-history-a0c7210b20faf0f53be44e8b"],"relevance":"Supplied official NYC Planning amendment-history snapshot listing the events, dates, CPC reports, report links, and notes for ZR § 42-00."},{"sectionID":"20017271","sourceIDs":["1d64e29a-5afa-44b8-beb2-f4a7426af2c9"],"relevance":"Current enacted ZR § 42-00 text, including its statement that Chapter 2 use provisions may be modified or superseded by specified special rules."}]}

{"answerText":"The maximum permitted residential FAR **cannot be determined** from the supplied facts because the property’s mapped zoning district has not been established. The proposed building’s ratio is **4.0 FAR** (40,000 sq ft ÷ 10,000 sq ft), but that calculation alone does not establish whether 4.0 FAR is permitted.

The Zoning Resolution makes the zoning maps—including amendments—part of the Resolution, and those maps establish district locations and boundaries. Accordingly, the controlling official zoning-map designation for this site must be identified before a residential FAR limit can be selected. (ZR § 11-14)

A Bronx location by itself does not establish a zoning district, special-district status, or a residential FAR entitlement. The supplied evidence does not provide the applicable residential-FAR rule for an unidentified district, so no maximum FAR or compliance conclusion can be given.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Zoning-map designation controls district location","explanation":"ZR § 11-14 provides that the locations and boundaries of districts established by the Resolution are shown on the zoning maps. The maps, including amendments, are incorporated into the Resolution. Because no mapped district for the site is supplied, the applicable district-specific residential FAR limit cannot be selected.","sectionID":"20018425","sourceIDs":["0932abe5-b659-42cc-93d3-d4ec13358258"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The controlling official zoning-map designation or verified mapped-district status for the site. A usable property identifier, such as its address or BBL, together with the controlling official zoning-map evidence would establish the needed map basis."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The assembled enacted evidence establishes that zoning maps control district location and boundaries, but it does not include a map designation for this site or a residential-FAR provision for the unidentified mapped district."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["The controlling official zoning map or verified mapped-district evidence for the identified property.","The enacted residential FAR provision applicable to the verified district, including any applicable special-district or other mapped overlay provisions."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20018425","sourceIDs":["0932abe5-b659-42cc-93d3-d4ec13358258"],"relevance":"ZR § 11-14 is the governing enacted passage establishing that zoning-map district locations and boundaries are incorporated into the Zoning Resolution."}]}
